"# aos-hook: offer hook points from your own program\n\nUse aos-hook when a program you write (C++, shell, anything that can start a process) should let others hook into it, the way an agent's hooks.json hooks into an agent. Hooking into an existing agent is `aos-agent hook` instead (aos/cli/hook.json).\n\nTwo things make a hook point: a declaration `hook_points.json` next to your program, and one `aos-hook fire` at that moment.\n\n```json\n{\"description\": \"...\", \"provider\": \"my-saver\",\n \"points\": {\"before_save\": {\"when\": \"before a file is saved\", \"phase\": \"before\",\n   \"fields\": {\"file\": \"the file name\"}, \"change\": {\"file\": \"nonempty_string\"},\n   \"block\": true, \"if_blocked\": \"the file is not saved\"}}}\n```\n\n```sh\nout=$(aos-hook fire --points hook_points.json --list config.json --stack \"$STATE\" --field file=\"$f\" before_save)\n# exit 0: out is {\"data\": {...}, \"changed\": [...], \"blocked\": false, \"hook\": null, \"text\": \"\"}; take .data.file\n# exit 10: a hook blocked it (text = that hook's stdout); exit 1: an error line is already on stderr\naos-hook fire -q ... before_save           # only the exit code\naos-hook points --points hook_points.json  # what the points are (-j for JSON)\naos-hook check --points hook_points.json --list config.json   # declarations and lists, no handler runs\n```\n\n- The list (`--list`, repeatable) is any JSON file with a `hooks` table, the same entries as an agent's hooks.json (event, handler, condition, intercept, async, on_error, timeout_ms). A later file overrides an earlier one by name, null turns a hook off, a missing file has no hooks.\n- Event data, later wins: stdin JSON object; a field declared with `{\"about\", \"env\", \"type\"}` takes that environment variable when it is set and not empty; `--field NAME=VALUE`; `--field-file NAME=PATH` (no file = null); `--field-default NAME=VALUE` (only when still missing or null). Give stdin JSON or /dev/null: aos-hook reads stdin to the end unless it is a terminal.\n- `--stack DIR` (default `$AOS_STACK`) is where each hook gets its own folder `DIR/hooks/NAME/`.\n- Hooks run in name order through the same engine agents use; a hook may change only the point's `change` keys, with that type. `--agent DEF_DIR` is not available yet.\n- Errors (`aos-hook: error: CODE reason`, exit 1): `HookFailed` (an on_error stop hook failed), `HooksBad`, `PointsBad`, `EventUnknown`, `ArgumentsBad`, `StackMissing`. A failing on_error continue hook is one warning, exit 0.\n- Each fire starts one Python process (tens of milliseconds) plus the handlers. More: `aos-hook --help`, `aos-hook fire --help`.\n"
